2186404258 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y,\\,x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0,\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4) \\\\  & =(t)(4)-4 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{2})-4 \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}-4)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(0,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(-2,4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(-2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,4)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(2,4).$" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404260 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404261 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404262 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404263 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404264 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1077 "selection" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1079 #edb/ref 17592186404264 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404259 1080 #uuid "24e0c5a8-3acd-57db-aba7-34b3ec30ab80" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404260 1081 "${f}_{min}=-10$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404260 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404260 1084 #uuid "cccc9be8-40ad-59d1-85aa-0c32e8379a16" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404261 1081 "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404261 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 