\lambda $ of the matrix \n$O=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404384 1072 #uuid "8b75d1ac-303a-511c-86a2-e86c1eaa7201" 13194139533495 17592186045585 true] [17592186404384 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,O-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nO-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(O-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(O-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1-\\lambda  \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1)+(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $2:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2\n$$\n\nLet's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}+2(1)^{2}-(1)-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the equation. So, we can factor $(\\lambda -1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=